spindle.N.start-inhibit is an ignition; spindle.N.inhibit is only a clutch.

spindle.N.inhibit was the pin a configuration reached for when it needed to
forbid the spindle, and it does not do that.  It zeroes the speed *scale*: an
M3 issued while it is held still succeeds, the spindle state stays on, the
status buffer keeps reporting the commanded speed -- and dropping the pin
spins the spindle straight back up with no operator action.  An interlock
whose release restarts the machine is the opposite of an interlock.

start-inhibit refuses the start outright, with a message naming the reason,
stops a spindle that is already turning, and leaves it stopped: releasing the
pin requires a new start command.  The test pins down all three, and in
particular that the release does not restart the spindle, which is the
property that separates the two pins.

An orient counts as a start.  M19 deliberately leaves the spindle state at 0
-- it is not an M3 -- but it opens the brake and hands the spindle to the
external orient loop, which turns it.  Checking only the run state would let
M19 through an interlock that says the spindle may not run, so the test issues
one under the pin and asserts the brake stays engaged.

The test also covers the enables bug both inhibits shared.  The guards read
"enables & *inhibit_pin", and since a bit pin is 0 or 1 that ANDed against
0x01 == SS_ENABLED: both inhibits silently stopped working whenever spindle
override was switched off from the UI.  Nothing in the suite would have
noticed, because the pin still reads TRUE while doing nothing.
